\section{A typical program}

    Every sum in Table \ref{tab:results} was made with the program below, which is in \file{programs/sums.p47}. It is the sum of that table: $1/n^2$
    for $n = 1$ to $10000$.

    \cmd{FSQ} is the term. The engine calls it once per count with $n$ in \reg{X}, and it leaves $1/n^2$ there for the engine to collect. \cmd{EUP} is
    the run: the three numbers, then the engine, naming \cmd{FSQ} as the term to call.

    \prog{LBL 'FSQ'\\\ \ 1/𝑥\\\ \ 𝑥²\\RTN\\\\LBL 'EUP'\\\ \ 1\\\ \ 10000\\\ \ 1\\\ \ Σₙ 'FSQ'\\RTN}

    From the keyboard it is the same three numbers, then \cmd{Σₙ} on the second soft key of \fkey{ADV}, then the program name at the prompt. Read the
    line as: from 1, to 10000, in steps of 1, do the sum.

    \keys{\key{1}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{1}\key{0}\key{0}\key{0}\key{0}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{1}\quad\fkey{ADV}\quad\mkey{Σₙ}\quad\vr{FSQ}}

    Counting down is the same three numbers the other way about, with the step made negative:

    \keys{\key{1}\key{0}\key{0}\key{0}\key{0}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{1}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{1}\quad\key{CHS}\quad\fkey{ADV}\quad\mkey{Σₙ}\quad\vr{FSQ}}

    The two \cmd{+} lines of Table \ref{tab:results} came from the same program with the end of \cmd{FSQ} changed. Instead of leaving the term for the
    engine, it adds the term to a variable of its own and hands the engine a zero, so the running total is \vr{SS} and the engine's 75 digits are
    never used:

    \prog{LBL 'CSQ'\\\ \ 1/𝑥\\\ \ 𝑥²\\\ \ RCL 'SS'\\\ \ +\\\ \ STO 'SS'\\\ \ 0\\RTN}

    The \cmd{CSQ} term program is a hack, using the looping of \cmd{Σₙ} but a register for every addition. It is not the usual way to write a sum. It
    is here to illustrate the loss of digits in a 34 digit run.

    Clear \vr{SS} before the run and read it afterwards. This is the version whose counting direction matters.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{In summary}

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Let \cmd{Σₙ} do the adding using a programmed sum, not a manual \cmd{STO+} or similar.
        \item \cmd{Σₙ} never stops early. Positive and negative count directions are both possible, both with fixed terms.
        \item \cmd{∞Σₙ} stops as soon as it is deemed the last digit cannot change any more, and counts up or down as \cmd{Σₙ} above.
    \end{itemize}

